      "lem:torus-cic-identification",
      "thm:nested-derivative"
    ],
    "justification": "The mixed high-times-low remainder and uniform triangular-array Lindeberg bound establish the full nested fixed-grid limit.",
    "gap": "Neither fixed-path differentiability nor the fixed-source uniform-law result supplies this shrinking-bandwidth four-input expansion.",
    "consumer": "It supports inference for prespecified bivariate displacement arrows.",
    "proof_tex": "By lem:torus-cic-identification, \\(\\tau(P)\\) is the causal no-treatment displacement, whereas def:plugin-estimator is an observed-law plug-in; it remains to prove their asymptotic relation.  On the good event in lem:uniform-critical-estimates, clipping is inactive and the selected branches agree with the population branches.  Apply the mixed expansion (24) there with \\(u_g=\\widetilde f_g-f_g\\).  Because displacement is the negative of the lifted map variation,\n\\[\n \\widehat\\tau-\\tau(P)\n =-\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1{n_g}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_g}\n \\Psi_{g,h_n}(X_{g,i})+b_n+R_n,\n\\tag{40}\n\\]\nwhere centering is included in \\(\\Psi_{g,h_n}\\), the order-\\(s\\) kernel moments and the order-minus-two map response give\n\\[\n \\lVert b_n\\rVert\\leq Ch_n^{s-2},\n\\tag{41}\n\\]\nand (16), (24), and Cauchy--Schwarz give\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_P\\lVert R_n\\rVert^2\n \\leq C(\\mathbb EU_{1,n}^4)^{1/2}(\\mathbb EU_{0,n}^4)^{1/2}\n \\leq Cq_{1,n}^2q_{0,n}^2.\n\\tag{42}\n\\]\nLem:mixed-bandwidth-feasibility makes (41) and (42) \\(o(\\sqrt{L_n/n})\\) and \\(o(L_n/n)\\), respectively.\n\nFor any fixed \\(t\\in\\mathbb R^{2J}\\), set\n\\[\n Z_{g,i,n}=-\\sqrt{\\frac n{L_n}}\\,n_g^{-1}\n t^{\\mathsf T}\\Psi_{g,h_n}(X_{g,i}).\n\\]\nThe four samples are independent and the scores are centered.  By the fourth-moment bound,\n\\[\n \\sum_{g,i}\\mathbb E_P|Z_{g,i,n}|^4\n \\leq\\frac{C\\lVert t\\rVert^4}{nL_n^2h_n^2}\\longrightarrow0,\n\\tag{43}\n\\]\nuniformly over \\(P\\), where the last limit is in lem:mixed-bandwidth-feasibility.  Taylor expansion of the characteristic functions, using (43) for the remainder, proves the scalar Lindeberg central limit theorem.  Its covariance is\n\\[\n \\frac1{L_n}\\sum_g\\frac n{n_g}\n \\operatorname{Var}_{P_g}(\\Psi_{g,h_n}(X)),\n\\tag{44}\n\\]\nwhich converges uniformly to \\(V(P)\\) by the cell-ratio assumption and lem:full-critical-covariance.  Applying the scalar conclusion to every \\(t\\) gives the finite-dimensional Gaussian limit.  Uniformity of (41)--(44), compactness of the uniformly bounded covariance set, and the bounded-Lipschitz characterization yield\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal F_s}d_{\\mathrm{BL}}\n \\left(\\mathcal L_P\\left[\\sqrt{n/L_n}(\\widehat\\tau-\\tau(P))\\right],\n N(0,V(P))\\right)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nFinally, (17), (20), and ass:lift-regularity show that both estimated maps remain on the selected half-open-cell branch with probability \\(1-o(1)\\), uniformly.  On its complement the estimator uses the fixed bounded fallback, whose probability is exponentially smaller than the critical scale by \\(n h_n^{13/2}/\\log n\\to\\infty\\).  Hence it does not change the limit.",
